<description>
Decode the two-row red glyph strip into a downward red trace starting from a centered green marker.

Input structure
- The input is a black 2-row strip containing 4-connected red glyphs, read left to right.
- Each glyph spans both rows and has exactly one top red entry cell; its bottom row is either the same cell only or a two-cell elbow.
Rule
- Make a black output whose width is one more than half the input width and whose height is one marker row plus one row for each elbow and two rows for each vertical glyph.
- Put a green marker in the center of the top row, then trace the glyphs downward: a vertical glyph paints two red cells in the current column; an elbow paints the two bottom cells as a horizontal red segment and moves the current column left or right.
Key insight
- The missing top cell of each 2x2 elbow tells whether the trace exits left or right; the glyphs are pipe instructions rather than shapes to copy literally.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "implicit trace head initialized at the centered green output marker",
  "path": "read the red input glyphs left to right; a vertical glyph advances two rows in the same column, and a 2x2 elbow advances one row while stepping to the bottom cell not under the entry cell",
  "trajectory": "paint the decoded vertical or horizontal red segment for each glyph",
  "transform": "build a black output canvas with the green marker at top center; do not copy the input strip itself",
  "grid_shape": "height is one marker row plus decoded path rows; width is input_width/2 + 1",
  "symbol_instructions": "each red glyph's single top cell is the entry; its bottom row defines the segment to paint and the next trace-head column"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  input:
    grid: 2 rows x W cols, W even, only black and red
    structure: sequence of N red glyphs separated by 1-column black gaps, read left-to-right
    glyph_types:
      vertical: 1-wide, both rows red ([[1],[1]]) — encodes "go straight down 2 rows"
      right_elbow: 2-wide, [[1,0],[1,1]] — encodes "step right"
      left_elbow: 2-wide, [[0,1],[1,1]] — encodes "step left"
    N: 5 [observed]
    num_vertical: 2 [observed], must be even so W is even (W = 3N - k - 1)
    num_elbows: 3 [observed]
  output:
    grid: (1 + 2*num_vertical + num_elbows) rows x (W/2 + 1) cols, black background
    top_row: single green cell at center column (out_w // 2)
    trace: red path descending from green marker; vertical glyph paints 2 cells in same column, elbow paints a 1x2 horizontal segment and shifts column by +-1
    trace_bounds: path column must stay in [0, out_w) at all times
  colors: input uses black(0) + red(2); output uses black(0) + red(2) + green(3)

generation_strategy:
  approach: path-first (generate glyph sequence ensuring trace stays in bounds, then encode as input strip)
  steps:
    - Choose N=5 glyphs, with num_vertical k=2 (to keep input_width=12 even)
    - Assign glyph types: pick 2 positions for vertical, remaining 3 are elbows
    - Compute out_w = (3*5 - 2 - 1)//2 + 1 = 7, start_col = 3
    - For each elbow, assign direction (+1 or -1); simulate trace column and reject if it exits [0, out_w-1]
    - Encode glyph sequence into 2xW input strip: place each glyph pattern left-to-right with 1-col black gap between them
